<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
<para>In the cards, for each system with no solution, change a single constant term so that there are infinitely many solutions to the system.</para>
</problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:
    </para>
    <para>
      Card 2: change 3 to 13; Card 5: change \(-12\) to 12.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Ready for More Question2-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
<para>For each system with infinitely many solutions, change a single constant term so that there are no solutions to the system.</para>
</problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:
    </para>
    <para>
      Any change to a constant term would work.
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Ready for More Question3-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
<para>Explain why in these situations it is impossible to change a single constant term so that there is exactly one solution to the system.</para>
</problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:
    </para>
    <para>
      Changing a constant term won’t change the slope of the lines, so the graphs will be either distinct parallel lines or the same line.

    </para>
  </solution></exercise></section>
